1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,870
Imagine waking up one morning
and discovering that your

2
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:05,060
body is no longer necessary.

3
00:00:06,203 --> 00:00:09,263
You can see, think,
remember your childhood,

4
00:00:09,273 --> 00:00:11,933
recognize your family
and have conversations.

5
00:00:13,216 --> 00:00:15,006
But there's one strange difference...

6
00:00:16,029 --> 00:00:18,439
You're no longer living
inside a human body.

7
00:00:18,559 --> 00:00:20,199
You're living inside a computer.

8
00:00:21,512 --> 00:00:22,852
Sounds impossible, right?

9
00:00:23,755 --> 00:00:27,145
But what if I told you that scientists
are already developing technologies that

10
00:00:27,155 --> 00:00:29,745
connect the human brain
directly to machines?

11
00:00:30,708 --> 00:00:34,028
And this raises one of the biggest
questions in human history...

12
00:00:34,901 --> 00:00:38,611
Could we someday upload our minds
and live forever digitally?

13
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:44,230
To understand this idea, we first need
to understand something extraordinary.

14
00:00:45,150 --> 00:00:48,550
Your brain is one of the most
complex structures known to science.

15
00:00:49,330 --> 00:00:52,820
It contains approximately
86 billion neurons.

16
00:00:53,610 --> 00:00:57,190
These neurons communicate through
electrical and chemical signals.

17
00:00:58,150 --> 00:01:03,070
Every memory, emotion, movement and thought
involves incredibly complex activity

18
00:01:03,080 --> 00:01:05,090
across networks of brain cells.

19
00:01:05,980 --> 00:01:10,460
When you remember your childhood, recognize
someone's face or imagine tomorrow,

20
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:14,790
your brain is processing information
through these interconnected networks.

21
00:01:16,060 --> 00:01:19,560
Scientists want to understand how
this information is represented.

22
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:24,780
Because if we could eventually map and
simulate the brain in enough detail,

23
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:27,940
could we recreate its
activity inside a computer?

24
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:32,370
This isn't entirely science fiction.

25
00:01:33,304 --> 00:01:37,514
Scientists have already developed something
called a brain-computer interface,

26
00:01:38,169 --> 00:01:40,259
commonly known as a BCI.

27
00:01:41,453 --> 00:01:45,253
These systems can detect and interpret
certain signals from the brain.

28
00:01:46,247 --> 00:01:50,797
In some experiments, people with severe
paralysis have used these technologies

29
00:01:50,807 --> 00:01:52,507
to control computer cursors,

30
00:01:53,232 --> 00:01:57,732
communicate and even interact with
digital devices using brain activity.

31
00:01:58,606 --> 00:02:01,546
Some systems use electrodes
placed on the scalp.

32
00:02:01,556 --> 00:02:04,746
Others use implanted electrodes
that record signals from

33
00:02:04,756 --> 00:02:06,226
much closer to the brain.

34
00:02:07,771 --> 00:02:09,971
Companies and research
laboratories are working to

35
00:02:09,981 --> 00:02:11,801
make these systems more capable.

36
00:02:12,715 --> 00:02:14,205
But here's something important.

37
00:02:15,229 --> 00:02:19,049
Reading certain brain signals is not
the same as reading every thought,

38
00:02:19,059 --> 00:02:20,309
memory or emotion.

39
00:02:21,484 --> 00:02:23,504
And connecting a brain to a computer

40
00:02:24,328 --> 00:02:28,128
is very different from
transferring an entire human mind.

41
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:33,310
Some brain-computer interfaces
use artificial intelligence

42
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:35,530
to decode patterns in brain signals.

43
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:39,780
Artificial neural networks are
simplified computational models,

44
00:02:39,970 --> 00:02:42,600
not complete copies of a biological brain.

45
00:02:45,400 --> 00:02:48,530
Now imagine taking this
technology much further.

46
00:02:49,351 --> 00:02:52,061
Instead of simply reading
signals from the brain,

47
00:02:52,742 --> 00:02:55,332
scientists would need to
understand its structure

48
00:02:55,342 --> 00:02:58,092
and activity in extraordinary detail.

49
00:02:59,353 --> 00:03:02,133
They would need to map the
connections between neurons,

50
00:03:02,854 --> 00:03:07,274
understand how memories are represented
and reproduce the brain's important

51
00:03:07,284 --> 00:03:09,814
processes in a computational system.

52
00:03:10,755 --> 00:03:14,615
This hypothetical technology is
often called whole-brain emulation.

53
00:03:14,685 --> 00:03:18,465
Think of it like creating a highly
detailed digital model of your brain.

54
00:03:19,556 --> 00:03:24,016
Your memories, personality, habits and
patterns of thinking would somehow need

55
00:03:24,026 --> 00:03:25,886
to be represented in that model.

56
00:03:27,046 --> 00:03:28,846
But there's a massive challenge.

57
00:03:29,957 --> 00:03:33,737
We still don't know enough about how all
these things work together to recreate

58
00:03:33,747 --> 00:03:35,477
an entire human mind.

59
00:03:36,428 --> 00:03:39,728
And even if we could create a
perfect simulation of your brain,

60
00:03:40,729 --> 00:03:43,379
would that simulation actually be you?

61
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:48,670
Now we arrive at the strangest
part of this entire idea.

62
00:03:49,678 --> 00:03:53,598
Imagine scientists successfully
creating a digital copy of your brain.

63
00:03:54,305 --> 00:03:55,815
It remembers your childhood.

64
00:03:56,373 --> 00:03:58,123
It recognizes your family.

65
00:03:58,690 --> 00:04:00,550
It speaks exactly like you.

66
00:04:01,188 --> 00:04:04,398
It even remembers conversations
that happened years ago.

67
00:04:05,276 --> 00:04:06,556
But here's the question...

68
00:04:07,343 --> 00:04:11,373
If your digital copy wakes up tomorrow,
does that mean you are still alive?

69
00:04:12,101 --> 00:04:15,021
Or have scientists simply
created another version of you?

70
00:04:15,031 --> 00:04:18,711
Imagine standing in front of a computer
and hearing your digital copy say:

71
00:04:19,518 --> 00:04:20,768
I remember everything.

72
00:04:21,576 --> 00:04:22,556
I am you.

73
00:04:22,636 --> 00:04:25,696
But your original body is
still standing right there.

74
00:04:25,806 --> 00:04:27,606
Which one is the real you?

75
00:04:29,174 --> 00:04:30,344
This is one of the biggest

76
00:04:30,354 --> 00:04:33,494
philosophical problems surrounding
digital consciousness.

77
00:04:34,681 --> 00:04:39,051
Copying someone's memories and
personality does not automatically prove

78
00:04:39,579 --> 00:04:43,979
that their original subjective experience
or consciousness has been transferred.

79
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:48,300
If scientists eventually
solve these problems,

80
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:51,700
the possibilities could be extraordinary.

81
00:04:53,180 --> 00:04:58,000
Imagine preserving someone's memories
and personality in a digital system.

82
00:04:59,470 --> 00:05:01,770
Imagine being able to
interact with a digital

83
00:05:01,780 --> 00:05:04,280
representation of someone who passed away.

84
00:05:05,840 --> 00:05:09,630
Imagine exploring virtual worlds
without a biological body.

85
00:05:11,170 --> 00:05:14,930
You could theoretically exist in
environments that look completely different

86
00:05:14,940 --> 00:05:16,250
from the physical world.

87
00:05:17,890 --> 00:05:19,780
But there are enormous challenges.

88
00:05:19,790 --> 00:05:22,220
How much computing power
would this require?

89
00:05:23,530 --> 00:05:26,400
How would we protect a
person's digital identity?

90
00:05:27,840 --> 00:05:30,740
Who would own the digital
version of someone's mind?

91
00:05:31,860 --> 00:05:34,070
And what happens if someone hacks it,

92
00:05:35,080 --> 00:05:37,760
changes its memories or shuts it down?

93
00:05:39,060 --> 00:05:43,830
Digital immortality could introduce
problems humanity has never faced before.

94
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:49,570
Despite all the excitement, we are still
very far away from uploading an entire

95
00:05:49,580 --> 00:05:51,500
human brain into a computer.

96
00:05:52,251 --> 00:05:55,601
The brain contains extraordinary
biological complexity.

97
00:05:56,343 --> 00:05:58,843
Scientists still have much
to learn about memory,

98
00:05:58,853 --> 00:06:03,453
consciousness and how the brain produces
our personal experience of being alive.

99
00:06:04,424 --> 00:06:06,234
Even mapping the brain in sufficient

100
00:06:06,244 --> 00:06:08,894
detail presents enormous
technical challenges.

101
00:06:09,846 --> 00:06:11,686
And there's another important distinction.

102
00:06:12,117 --> 00:06:16,377
A computer simulation that behaves like
you might not necessarily experience the

103
00:06:16,387 --> 00:06:17,557
world the way you do.

104
00:06:18,598 --> 00:06:22,548
We don't currently have a proven
scientific method for transferring human

105
00:06:22,558 --> 00:06:26,218
consciousness from a biological
brain into a digital system.

106
00:06:26,870 --> 00:06:32,472
So for now, digital immortality remains
a fascinating possibility rather than an

107
00:06:32,482 --> 00:06:33,982
available technology.

108
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:38,770
So, what if scientists eventually
discover how to upload the

109
00:06:38,780 --> 00:06:40,440
human brain into a computer?

110
00:06:40,810 --> 00:06:41,660
Would you do it?

111
00:06:42,140 --> 00:06:44,590
Would you leave your biological
body behind and take

112
00:06:44,600 --> 00:06:46,650
a chance on digital immortality?

113
00:06:47,180 --> 00:06:51,260
Or would you rather live a normal human
life, knowing that it eventually comes

114
00:06:51,270 --> 00:06:52,030
to an end?

115
00:06:52,900 --> 00:06:55,410
Perhaps the biggest question
isn't whether technology

116
00:06:55,420 --> 00:06:57,090
can recreate the human brain.

117
00:06:57,310 --> 00:07:02,040
It's whether a digital version of you
could ever truly experience being you.

118
00:07:02,550 --> 00:07:03,380
What do you think?

119
00:07:03,440 --> 00:07:06,110
Would you upload your
mind into a computer?

120
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:07,490
Let us know in the comments.

121
00:07:07,670 --> 00:07:10,510
And subscribe to AI Uncovered,
where we explore the

122
00:07:10,520 --> 00:07:13,830
technology that could
change humanity forever.
